(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-statistics-causation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Statistics, Correlation and Causation Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does statistical significance describe?","choices":["How unlikely it is that the result arose by chance","How much the treatment helps an individual patient","How carefully the study was designed and carried out","Whether the result would change clinical practice"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so a more statistically significant result is less likely to be chance and a less significant one is more likely to be chance. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does multivariable modeling examine?","choices":["The effect of a single variable in isolation","The order in which patients enter a study","The reliability of one repeated measurement","The effect of two or more variables together"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: it uses a mathematical construct, an equation, to examine variables together, including their combined effect in predicting a clinical event. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How is causality defined?","choices":["Any factor associated with an outcome","A factor that helps produce the outcome","Any factor that occurs before an outcome","Any factor with a significant p value"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: causality exists if a factor produces or contributes to the production of a specified outcome, which allows for multiple causes. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A trial reports a p value of 0.20. Which statement is best supported?","choices":["The treatment has been shown to have no effect","Eighty percent of patients improved","The result is very highly significant","The result is not statistically significant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since 0.20 is above the threshold of 0.05, so chance remains a reasonable explanation for the difference seen. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How is strength of association judged in a causal assessment?","choices":["By how many studies report it","By whether a mechanism is known","By the size of the relative risk","By whether exposure can be reversed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a large relative risk is a strong association, which makes a causal relationship more likely. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Researchers hope to show that a new drug lowers blood pressure more than placebo. What is the null hypothesis?","choices":["The drug lowers pressure more than placebo","The drug and placebo lower pressure equally","The placebo lowers pressure more than the drug","The drug lowers pressure in every patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the null hypothesis is that there is no difference, which is the opposite of what the researchers hope to find. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Disease X never occurs without exposure Y, but many exposed people stay healthy. What kind of cause is Y?","choices":["Necessary but not sufficient","Sufficient but not necessary","Both necessary and sufficient","Neither necessary nor sufficient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the disease cannot occur if Y is absent (necessary), but Y being present does not guarantee disease (not sufficient). — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does a p value express?","choices":["The probability that the treatment truly works","Chance of such data if no true difference","The size of the difference between groups","The probability that the study is biased"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, calculated on the assumption that no true difference exists, so it is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true or that the treatment works. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What can happen when many comparisons are made in one study?","choices":["Every p value becomes more accurate","Type II errors become impossible","Confidence intervals become narrower","Some may look significant by chance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the strength of statistical tests depends on the number of questions asked and how focused they are, and it is not always possible to know how many comparisons were made. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How is random error accounted for in a study?","choices":["By blinding the participants","Through the p values used","By matching cases with controls","By excluding older participants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: random error occurs by chance and is accounted for through the p values, whereas bias and confounding require design and analysis. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A study makes 200 comparisons, none reflecting a true difference. If significance is set at a p value of 0.01, about how many will appear significant by chance?","choices":["10","20","2","0"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a p value of 0.01 corresponds to a chance of 1 in 100, and 200 divided by 100 is 2. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which bias arises from difficulty remembering past exposures?","choices":["Recall bias","Selection bias","Measurement bias","Publication bias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: participants' difficulty in remembering distorts the reported exposure or history. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What happens to the evidence for causation as more criteria are met?","choices":["Causation is proven beyond doubt","The case for a cause grows stronger","The case for a cause grows weaker","The case for a cause is unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the more criteria for causation that are met, the stronger the case, though causation cannot be proved beyond all doubt. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which study limitation can produce a type II error?","choices":["A p value smaller than 0.001","Random assignment of participants","Blinding the investigators","Short follow-up or poor compliance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: too brief a follow-up or subjects not adhering can hide a real difference, so the result looks not significant though it is a false negative. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A trial of 40,000 patients finds a drug lowers systolic blood pressure by 1 millimeter of mercury, with a p value below 0.0001. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Clinically important but not statistically significant","Statistically significant but of doubtful clinical importance","Neither statistically nor clinically significant","Both statistically and clinically important"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: small differences can be statistically significant, especially in very large samples, yet a fall this small is unlikely to matter to patients. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A small trial of a drug that truly works reports a p value of 0.30. Which explanation is most plausible?","choices":["Too little power to detect the difference","The drug has been proven ineffective","A type I error has occurred","The null hypothesis has been proven true"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a small study has a low probability of detecting a real difference, so a false negative (type II error) is likely. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"On what does the validity of a multivariable model depend?","choices":["Whether its assumptions about the data hold","How many patients were recruited","Whether every variable reaches significance","How many variables are entered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: its validity rests on assumptions about the data, which commonly include assumptions such as normality and independence of errors. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What defines a sufficient cause?","choices":["If it is present, the disease always occurs","If it is absent, the disease cannot occur","If it is present, the disease sometimes occurs","If it is absent, the disease occurs less often"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a sufficient cause precedes the disease and guarantees it whenever present. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A short trial finds no significant benefit from a drug that truly works. Which error occurred?","choices":["Type I error","No error","Type II error","Recall bias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a real difference was missed, a false negative, which can result from limits such as short follow-up. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which test compares two means?","choices":["Fisher exact test","Student t test","Mann-Whitney U test","Chi-square test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, used between two means; the F test extends this to two or more means. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which variables are considered first for inclusion in a model?","choices":["Those least associated with the outcome","Those easiest to measure in every patient","Those with the most missing data","Those most strongly associated with the outcome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: after identifying variables related to the outcome, including confounders and effect modifiers, those with the strongest association are considered for inclusion. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A difference has a p value below 0.0001. What follows about its relevance to clinical practice?","choices":["It must be highly relevant to practice","It is relevant only if the sample was small","It is irrelevant because the p value is too low","Nothing; it may have no relevance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the p value speaks to chance, not to importance, so a difference with a p value below 0.0001 can still be irrelevant to practice. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"When the data offer little support for the null hypothesis, what gains support?","choices":["That the study is free of bias","That the groups were identical","That a difference is present","That the sample was adequate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: evidence against the null hypothesis, which states no difference, is what lends support to a difference being present. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does a 95 percent confidence interval give?","choices":["A range that should contain the true value","The range holding 95 percent of patients' values","The range in which the p value must fall","The range of bias in the estimate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the boundaries are the confidence limits, and if the study were repeated many times about 95 percent of such intervals would contain the true value. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The probability that a sampled patient has a condition is 0.35. What is the probability that a sampled patient does not have it?","choices":["0.35","0.50","1.35","0.65"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the probabilities of all possible events in the sample must sum to one, so the complement of 0.35 is 0.65. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does random assignment strengthen causal conclusions?","choices":["It removes all random error from results","It balances confounders between the groups","It guarantees a significant result","It makes measurements more reliable"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: random assignment spreads confounding factors evenly, although random error remains and is still accounted for through p values. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is a type I error?","choices":["Stating there is no difference when there is","Finding a difference that truly exists","Stating there is a difference when there is not","Measuring the outcome incorrectly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a false positive, also called an alpha error, which is the error most medical research is concerned with and is judged through the p value. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is a type II error?","choices":["Stating there is a difference when there is not","Finding no difference where none exists","Stating there is no difference when there is","Choosing the wrong statistical test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a false negative, also called a beta error, in which a real difference is missed and the result appears not significant. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why is a cross-sectional study weak evidence for causation?","choices":["It always has too few participants","It does not measure the outcome","It cannot show which came first","It cannot detect any association"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: exposure and outcome are measured at the same time, so the temporal sequence, an essential criterion for cause, cannot be established. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A trial reports a p value of 0.03 for a new drug against placebo. Which statement is best supported?","choices":["The difference is statistically significant","There is a 3 percent chance the drug does not work","The drug works in 97 percent of patients","The difference is large enough to change practice"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because 0.03 falls below the generally accepted threshold of 0.05, making chance an unlikely explanation. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
